1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,840
 Welcome to chapter three, Nutrients, Membrains and Transport.

2
00:00:06,840 --> 00:00:11,840
 In this chapter, we're going to look at those concepts, but we're also going to be asking

3
00:00:11,840 --> 00:00:17,920
 the question, "Is it possible to supplement your way to better performance and health?"

4
00:00:17,920 --> 00:00:23,480
 We live in a world today where supplements and vitamins and other nutrients are readily

5
00:00:23,480 --> 00:00:30,400
 available to buy separately from the foods that we eat, but do they really work?

6
00:00:30,400 --> 00:00:35,600
 Do they make us healthier and do they make us perform better?

7
00:00:35,600 --> 00:00:43,800
 Ginkgo to improve memory, Kava to reduce stress, ginseng to boost energy, and melatonin to

8
00:00:43,800 --> 00:00:45,720
 help you sleep.

9
00:00:45,720 --> 00:00:48,920
 Sounds like a recipe for success, doesn't it?

10
00:00:48,920 --> 00:00:54,520
 For good measure, chase those supplements down with some coconut water to slow aging

11
00:00:54,520 --> 00:00:56,240
 and prevent cancer.

12
00:00:56,240 --> 00:01:03,280
 You might have heard claims like this before, vitamins, minerals, herbs, yeast, and even

13
00:01:03,280 --> 00:01:11,000
 enzymes that are claiming to help you be healthier or to perform better in different tasks in

14
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:12,700
 your life.

15
00:01:12,700 --> 00:01:17,360
 And another positive with these things is that they come in a bottle and they can stay

16
00:01:17,360 --> 00:01:23,160
 on your shelf and stay good with a longer expiration date than the fresh foods that you

17
00:01:23,160 --> 00:01:27,240
 might buy from a grocery store.

18
00:01:27,240 --> 00:01:31,600
 Instead of going to a store every weekend and throwing away food that you never get

19
00:01:31,600 --> 00:01:37,080
 around to eating, you could stock your pantry with things like energy drinks, vitamin enriched

20
00:01:37,080 --> 00:01:43,760
 waters, protein powders, nutrition bars, and other bottles of vitamins and minerals.

21
00:01:43,760 --> 00:01:49,800
 You could even buy them in bulk if you like because they won't rot like fruits and vegetables.

22
00:01:49,800 --> 00:01:57,880
 But are they as good for you as you might think or as the advertisement might say?

23
00:01:57,880 --> 00:02:03,400
 Is it possible to supplement your way to enhance academic performance and even better

24
00:02:03,400 --> 00:02:06,640
 health and even physical performance?

25
00:02:06,640 --> 00:02:14,320
 It seems most Americans think so because we spend around $6 billion a year in America

26
00:02:14,320 --> 00:02:22,080
 on these items and more than 2/3 of us are taking at least one type of supplement.

27
00:02:22,080 --> 00:02:27,920
 But let's investigate whether these products are really doing what we hope that they're

28
00:02:27,920 --> 00:02:35,080
 doing and while we're asking that question, we'll learn about biological concepts like

29
00:02:35,080 --> 00:02:41,040
 nutrients, cells, and membrane transport.

30
00:02:41,040 --> 00:02:42,740
 Here's our learning outcomes.

31
00:02:42,740 --> 00:02:47,240
 As stated in the textbook of this chapter, be sure to read through them and recognize

32
00:02:47,240 --> 00:02:54,400
 them as you come across them in your notes in your reading.

33
00:02:54,400 --> 00:03:01,280
 Do you use any of these types of supplements like ginkgo, cava, ginseng, melatonin, or

34
00:03:01,280 --> 00:03:08,000
 coconut water, or do you use some other supplements that are not on this list?

35
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:15,480
 Well like we said, American spends about $6 billion per year on supplements and 2/3 of

36
00:03:15,480 --> 00:03:20,800
 us are taking at least one.

37
00:03:20,800 --> 00:03:25,760
 Well in order to figure out if these things are actually working and what the body needs

38
00:03:25,760 --> 00:03:31,840
 to be healthy and to perform well, let's first look at nutrients.

39
00:03:31,840 --> 00:03:38,000
 First of all macronutrients, mackerel again means big, so these are nutrients that come

40
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:44,760
 in a large form that your body would have to digest first in order to make use of them.

41
00:03:44,760 --> 00:03:48,560
 Digest means to break them down into smaller particles.

42
00:03:48,560 --> 00:03:54,000
 Nutrients are substances that come in the foods that we eat and after we break them down

43
00:03:54,000 --> 00:04:01,600
 by digestion it provides a structural material for the body to build other things or it provides

44
00:04:01,600 --> 00:04:05,920
 the body with energy to do work.

45
00:04:05,920 --> 00:04:12,680
 Macronutrients are required in large amounts and they include water, carbohydrates, proteins,

46
00:04:12,680 --> 00:04:16,920
 and fats.

47
00:04:16,920 --> 00:04:22,280
 We studied water in our last chapter and we learned about properties of water.

48
00:04:22,280 --> 00:04:30,320
 Water consists of one atom of oxygen and two atoms of hydrogen combined together by covalent

49
00:04:30,320 --> 00:04:37,280
 bonds that are really strong, but also remember they are combined in a way in which oxygen

50
00:04:37,280 --> 00:04:42,240
 gets to spend a little more time with those electrons that they're sharing with the hydrogens

51
00:04:42,240 --> 00:04:44,840
 because oxygen is more electronegative.

52
00:04:44,840 --> 00:04:51,200
 So it causes a polarity to the molecule where oxygen has a net negative charge and hydrogen

53
00:04:51,200 --> 00:04:53,960
 has a net positive charge.

54
00:04:53,960 --> 00:05:00,100
 So because of the polarity of a water molecule, charges on opposite ends, it sets up that

55
00:05:00,100 --> 00:05:07,280
 molecule to have some properties that we discussed last chapter such as being a great solvent

56
00:05:07,280 --> 00:05:13,240
 to dissolve ions and other molecules, it's the universal solvent, doesn't dissolve everything

57
00:05:13,240 --> 00:05:16,240
 but it does dissolve a lot of things.

58
00:05:16,240 --> 00:05:23,000
 So in the body we say it disperses nutrients really well and we're made up of a large percentage

59
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:29,560
 of water so all of those nutrients that we have are within that water, within our cells

60
00:05:29,560 --> 00:05:35,480
 and also within the extracellular fluid as well.

61
00:05:35,480 --> 00:05:43,560
 Water molecules also like to stick together, that's called cohesion when that happens.

62
00:05:43,560 --> 00:05:48,520
 Even also remember because of the hydrogen bonds between water molecules, those bonds

63
00:05:48,520 --> 00:05:54,720
 have to be broken first before water will change its speed and such when it's heating

64
00:05:54,720 --> 00:06:01,960
 up or cooling down, so water resists temperatures, temperature changes, so it's a really good

65
00:06:01,960 --> 00:06:08,800
 regulator for temperature for our body and for our environment and those are all good

66
00:06:08,800 --> 00:06:16,280
 things about it, it dissolves and eliminates waste products as well.

67
00:06:16,280 --> 00:06:21,040
 So how many liters of water are lost by an average human each day?

68
00:06:21,040 --> 00:06:28,840
 Well the answer there is actually three liters.

69
00:06:28,840 --> 00:06:35,280
 Through sweating, urinating and in feces, three liters are lost each day.

70
00:06:35,280 --> 00:06:40,240
 One and a half liters will be replaced by the foods we eat and one and a half will need

71
00:06:40,240 --> 00:06:46,520
 to be replaced from other sources.

72
00:06:46,520 --> 00:06:54,240
 While we can survive without food for several weeks, survival without water is limited to

73
00:06:54,240 --> 00:06:57,360
 just a few days.

74
00:06:57,360 --> 00:07:02,880
 When you haven't had enough water, you get dehydrated.

75
00:07:02,880 --> 00:07:06,160
 One is decreased water intake.

76
00:07:06,160 --> 00:07:10,160
 You might even start feeling symptoms when you haven't had enough water.

77
00:07:10,160 --> 00:07:15,120
 Minor symptoms include muscle cramps, fatigue, headache, dizziness, nausea, confusion and

78
00:07:15,120 --> 00:07:18,720
 even an increased heart rate.

79
00:07:18,720 --> 00:07:23,840
 That last one is due to a decreased blood volume when you're dehydrated so your heart

80
00:07:23,840 --> 00:07:31,600
 tries to compensate for that effect by speeding up as it tries to maintain homeostasis.

81
00:07:31,600 --> 00:07:37,880
 When you're severely dehydrated, things like hallucinations, heat stroke and even death

82
00:07:37,880 --> 00:07:42,320
 can result.

83
00:07:42,320 --> 00:07:45,560
 One of the major ways that you lose water is through sweating.

84
00:07:45,560 --> 00:07:51,360
 It's the evaporation of water from the skin and it helps to cool the body down by releasing

85
00:07:51,360 --> 00:07:55,480
 energy as the water molecules leave your skin.

86
00:07:55,480 --> 00:07:58,440
 It helps you maintain your body temperature.

87
00:07:58,440 --> 00:08:04,560
 When water is low and sweating decreases, body temperature can rise to a harmful level.

88
00:08:04,560 --> 00:08:13,840
 Therefore, you need to replace what you lose.

89
00:08:13,840 --> 00:08:19,440
 Although it works quite well to replace the water with tap water, many people drink bottled

90
00:08:19,440 --> 00:08:23,960
 water, in part because of concerns about the quality of tap water.

91
00:08:23,960 --> 00:08:30,920
 However, the U.S. Food and Drug Administration, the FDA, the government agency that sets standards

92
00:08:30,920 --> 00:08:37,000
 for bottled water is responsible for ensuring food safety in general and uses the same standards

93
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:44,400
 applied by the Environmental Protection Agency or the EPA to ensure that tap water is helpful.

94
00:08:44,400 --> 00:08:48,560
 In other words, water from both sources should be equally clean.

95
00:08:48,560 --> 00:08:59,940
 In fact, nearly 40% of bottled water actually contains water just from municipal tap water.

96
00:08:59,940 --> 00:09:05,840
 Many people might choose to rehydrate themselves with sports drinks.

97
00:09:05,840 --> 00:09:10,400
 But studies have shown that hydrating with water is actually better for athletes than

98
00:09:10,400 --> 00:09:13,840
 hydrating with costly sports drinks.

99
00:09:13,840 --> 00:09:21,240
 So sports drinks can come with other additives, such as sugars that provide unneeded calories

100
00:09:21,240 --> 00:09:28,320
 or additives that have unproven benefits, and it costs more than water.

101
00:09:28,320 --> 00:09:37,360
 Yes, bottled water and other sports drinks come in bottles that are convenient and portable,

102
00:09:37,360 --> 00:09:40,200
 but it does come at a cost to the environment.

103
00:09:40,200 --> 00:09:47,080
 Those billions of bottles used each year require 1.5 million barrels of oil to produce.

104
00:09:47,080 --> 00:09:54,600
 Although the bottles are recyclable, 86% of those bottles go to landfills each year.

105
00:09:54,600 --> 00:09:59,960
 In addition, the energy required to transport water FAR exceeds the energy required to

106
00:09:59,960 --> 00:10:09,200
 purify an equivalent amount of just plain old tap water.

107
00:10:09,200 --> 00:10:14,880
 Besides obtaining a healthy dose of water each day, people need to consume foods that

108
00:10:14,880 --> 00:10:22,860
 contain macronutrients like carbohydrates, proteins, and fats.

109
00:10:22,860 --> 00:10:29,160
 In our last chapter, we discussed those in more detail and their structures.

110
00:10:29,160 --> 00:10:36,320
 On of those, what molecules are the major source of energy for ourselves?

111
00:10:36,320 --> 00:10:42,160
 The source of energy that allows us to create our very own energy molecules called ATP.

112
00:10:42,160 --> 00:10:50,040
 Well, the answer to that is carbohydrates.

113
00:10:50,040 --> 00:10:54,920
 Carbohydrates are a macronutrient and our main energy source.

114
00:10:54,920 --> 00:11:01,520
 It's the main energy source that we use to quickly produce ATP, our own body's energy

115
00:11:01,520 --> 00:11:05,440
 molecule.

116
00:11:05,440 --> 00:11:10,680
 It's relatively easy to get the amount of carbohydrates that we need each day in our

117
00:11:10,680 --> 00:11:11,680
 diet.

118
00:11:11,680 --> 00:11:17,960
 They come in food sources that tend to be relatively easy to prepare and to obtain,

119
00:11:17,960 --> 00:11:26,880
 such as bread, cereals, rice, pasta, fruits, and vegetables.

120
00:11:26,880 --> 00:11:31,520
 Remember that carbohydrates are a macromolecule, they're very large, and they're made out

121
00:11:31,520 --> 00:11:37,800
 of their monomer or repeating subunits called simple sugars.

122
00:11:37,800 --> 00:11:43,000
 When it's just one simple sugar by itself, it's called a monosaccharide, and they can

123
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:49,480
 also be made out of long chains of these simple sugars, and when it's that case, it's called

124
00:11:49,480 --> 00:11:52,600
 the polysaccharide.

125
00:11:52,600 --> 00:11:58,080
 The single unit simple sugars are digested, and they enter the bloodstream quickly after

126
00:11:58,080 --> 00:11:59,800
 you eat them.

127
00:11:59,800 --> 00:12:05,640
 More sugars found in many of the foods we eat, milk, juice, honey, and most of the refined

128
00:12:05,640 --> 00:12:17,840
 foods that we eat contain branching chains that are called complex carbohydrates.

129
00:12:17,840 --> 00:12:23,200
 Complex carbohydrates are found in fruits, vegetables, breads, legumes, and in pasta as

130
00:12:23,200 --> 00:12:27,480
 well.

131
00:12:27,480 --> 00:12:34,560
 Small simple sugars, like glucose and fructose, are digested into the bloodstream very quickly.

132
00:12:34,560 --> 00:12:39,940
 The body digests complex carbohydrates much more slowly than it does those simpler sugars

133
00:12:39,940 --> 00:12:45,280
 because the complex carbohydrates have more chemical bonds, and then thus would need to

134
00:12:45,280 --> 00:12:49,600
 have more digestion break them down.

135
00:12:49,600 --> 00:12:55,000
 Endurance athletes often load up on complex carbohydrates for several days before a race

136
00:12:55,000 --> 00:13:00,120
 to increase the amount of stored energy that will be available during their competition.

137
00:13:00,120 --> 00:13:08,120
 Ideally, we would hope the carbohydrates would be from food rather than dietary supplements.

138
00:13:08,120 --> 00:13:12,760
 Nutritionists agree that most of the carbohydrates in a helpful diet should be in the form of

139
00:13:12,760 --> 00:13:19,680
 complex carbohydrates, and that it's best to consume only minimal amounts of processed

140
00:13:19,680 --> 00:13:21,840
 sugars.

141
00:13:21,840 --> 00:13:28,200
 It is a processed food, you might ask, well, it's refined foods that are stripped of their

142
00:13:28,200 --> 00:13:31,840
 nutritional value.

143
00:13:31,840 --> 00:13:37,600
 They've gone through a process of extensive refinement, and therefore, some are much of

144
00:13:37,600 --> 00:13:41,000
 the nutrient value has been stripped as well.

145
00:13:41,000 --> 00:13:46,840
 For example, unrefined raw brown sugar is made from the juice of a sugar cane plant, and

146
00:13:46,840 --> 00:13:52,280
 it contains minerals and nutrients that are also found in the plant.

147
00:13:52,280 --> 00:13:58,520
 Processing brown sugar produces refined brown sugar, like the kind most of us buy at a grocery

148
00:13:58,520 --> 00:14:08,200
 store, and that results in the loss of those vitamins and minerals.

149
00:14:08,200 --> 00:14:16,440
 Foods that haven't been stripped of their nutrition by processing are called whole foods.

150
00:14:16,440 --> 00:14:22,200
 Whole grains, beans, and many fruits and vegetables are whole foods, and in addition to being nutritious,

151
00:14:22,200 --> 00:14:29,240
 whole foods tend to be good sources of what you might call dietary fiber.

152
00:14:29,240 --> 00:14:32,800
 Sometimes we also call fiber roughage.

153
00:14:32,800 --> 00:14:35,920
 Fiber is an important part of a healthy diet.

154
00:14:35,920 --> 00:14:44,280
 It's composed mainly of the complex carbohydrates that humans can't digest, such as plants have

155
00:14:44,280 --> 00:14:47,760
 cell walls surrounding their cells.

156
00:14:47,760 --> 00:14:53,520
 These cell walls are made out of a sugar called cellulose, and we don't have any enzymes to

157
00:14:53,520 --> 00:14:55,640
 digest that cellulose.

158
00:14:55,640 --> 00:15:00,840
 So you might ask, "Well, if we can't digest it, why do we eat this then?"

159
00:15:00,840 --> 00:15:09,720
 Well, we call that cellulose fiber or roughage, and it stays in our digestive tract and passes

160
00:15:09,720 --> 00:15:16,440
 into our large intestine, it also helps to draw water into the digestive system as it's

161
00:15:16,440 --> 00:15:21,120
 passing.

162
00:15:21,120 --> 00:15:27,640
 This fiber and roughage gives bulk to our feces.

163
00:15:27,640 --> 00:15:33,080
 Fiber is not a nutrient because it's not absorbed by the body, but as you can see from the description

164
00:15:33,080 --> 00:15:39,040
 I just gave you, it is part of a healthful diet and helps to keep an individual regular

165
00:15:39,040 --> 00:15:44,240
 with their bowel movements, helps you also to maintain healthy cholesterol levels, and

166
00:15:44,240 --> 00:15:47,840
 might even decrease your risk for certain cancers.

167
00:15:47,840 --> 00:15:54,560
 Well, what if you don't like things like lettuce and green leafy vegetables, which contain

168
00:15:54,560 --> 00:15:56,040
 a lot of fiber?

169
00:15:56,040 --> 00:15:57,960
 How about fiber bars?

170
00:15:57,960 --> 00:16:01,360
 We tend to see a lot of those on grocery store shelves.

171
00:16:01,360 --> 00:16:07,880
 Yes, they may be rich in different types of fiber, but usually they're also rich in processed

172
00:16:07,880 --> 00:16:14,160
 sugars and other additives that have a non-nutritive value, therefore it's best to get your fiber

173
00:16:14,160 --> 00:16:17,400
 from whole foods.

174
00:16:17,400 --> 00:16:22,640
 Our next macronutrient is proteins, another one of those macromolecules that you need

175
00:16:22,640 --> 00:16:28,880
 daily to be healthy proteins, make up parts of cellular structures, and also they're so

176
00:16:28,880 --> 00:16:36,600
 important they take part in most body processes, such as when they act like enzymes, to make

177
00:16:36,600 --> 00:16:39,680
 certain reactions occur within the body.

178
00:16:39,680 --> 00:16:48,200
 They're so important that our DNA codes for proteins, and then those proteins make you.

179
00:16:48,200 --> 00:16:53,280
 Food sources where you can get them include beef, poultry, fish, beans, eggs, nuts, and

180
00:16:53,280 --> 00:17:01,160
 dairy products like milk, yogurt, and cheeses.

181
00:17:01,160 --> 00:17:06,720
 In our last chapter we discussed the structural composition of proteins.

182
00:17:06,720 --> 00:17:12,320
 Remember the repeating subunit, or monomer unit, that proteins are made from are called

183
00:17:12,320 --> 00:17:18,040
 amino acids, and that there's about 20 different amino acids that make up the proteins that

184
00:17:18,040 --> 00:17:21,520
 are in our body.

185
00:17:21,520 --> 00:17:28,200
 DNA codes for those amino acids to be chained in the right order, and then other cell components

186
00:17:28,200 --> 00:17:34,720
 will fold those chains of amino acids into just the right shape so those proteins can

187
00:17:34,720 --> 00:17:39,200
 do the job that they need to do.

188
00:17:39,200 --> 00:17:45,600
 From what you eat, your body is actually able to synthesize or assemble many of the commonly

189
00:17:45,600 --> 00:17:50,320
 occurring amino acids that our proteins are made out of.

190
00:17:50,320 --> 00:17:56,160
 There are some, however, out of those 20 amino acids that our bodies cannot synthesize on

191
00:17:56,160 --> 00:17:57,720
 their own.

192
00:17:57,720 --> 00:18:01,720
 These are called essential amino acids.

193
00:18:01,720 --> 00:18:10,600
 You must get these by eating the proper foods.

194
00:18:10,600 --> 00:18:17,120
 Complete proteins contain all the essential amino acids that your body needs.

195
00:18:17,120 --> 00:18:23,700
 Proteins obtained by eating meat are probably the most likely to be complete than those

196
00:18:23,700 --> 00:18:26,440
 obtained by eating plants.

197
00:18:26,440 --> 00:18:32,220
 Therefore, if you're vegetarian, it's good to eat a variety of different types of plants

198
00:18:32,220 --> 00:18:41,040
 and beans so that you're sure to get those essential amino acids.

199
00:18:41,040 --> 00:18:46,680
 True or false, adding protein powder to your post-workout snack is always a healthy way

200
00:18:46,680 --> 00:18:49,400
 to add muscle.

201
00:18:49,400 --> 00:18:53,400
 Actually that answer is false.

202
00:18:53,400 --> 00:18:58,560
 People looking to gain muscle mass will sometimes supplement their diet with protein powders.

203
00:18:58,560 --> 00:19:04,160
 That can be mixed with milk or water, and it supposedly helps them rebuild protein-rich

204
00:19:04,160 --> 00:19:06,840
 muscle after a strenuous workout.

205
00:19:06,840 --> 00:19:11,560
 However, extra protein does not always lead to extra muscle.

206
00:19:11,560 --> 00:19:19,280
 Just like any other nutrient in the body, excess protein is stored as, you guessed it,

207
00:19:19,280 --> 00:19:20,280
 fat.

208
00:19:20,280 --> 00:19:28,040
 So, if you eat too much protein, you'll just stand up increasing your body fat content.

209
00:19:28,040 --> 00:19:33,400
 In addition, diets that are too rich in protein can lead to health problems like bone loss

210
00:19:33,400 --> 00:19:35,040
 and kidney damage.

211
00:19:35,040 --> 00:19:40,040
 If you're looking to build muscle, the most sensible thing to do is to combine your workout

212
00:19:40,040 --> 00:19:42,600
 regimen with a healthy diet.

213
00:19:42,600 --> 00:19:48,560
 Even the most active people, like endurance athletes, can easily obtain enough protein

214
00:19:48,560 --> 00:20:04,900
 by just having a healthy regular diet.

215
00:20:04,900 --> 00:20:09,280
 Fats are our next macronutrient.

216
00:20:09,280 --> 00:20:11,480
 Fat is stored, energy.

217
00:20:11,480 --> 00:20:16,680
 In fact, gram-for-gram fat contains about two times the amount of energy as carbohydrates

218
00:20:16,680 --> 00:20:19,240
 are protein-doped.

219
00:20:19,240 --> 00:20:25,920
 Foods that are rich in fat include meat, milk, cheeses, vegetable oils, and nuts.

220
00:20:25,920 --> 00:20:32,720
 Most mammals, including us humans, store fat just below the skin, which helps protect our

221
00:20:32,720 --> 00:20:39,120
 vital organs and give us insulation from those cold Minnesota winters, and also a great place

222
00:20:39,120 --> 00:20:43,000
 to store energy just in case famines should occur.

223
00:20:47,680 --> 00:20:56,880
 In our last chapter we discussed the composition of fat in its most common form as triglycerides

224
00:20:56,880 --> 00:21:03,720
 consists of a molecule of glycerol, which has three carbon atoms, attached to a number

225
00:21:03,720 --> 00:21:07,720
 of between one, two, or three fatty acid tails.

226
00:21:07,720 --> 00:21:12,800
 Those fatty acid tails consist of carbon and hydrogen bonded together and have a lot of

227
00:21:12,800 --> 00:21:18,800
 potential energy for the body.

228
00:21:18,800 --> 00:21:23,400
 Our body can synthesize most of the fatty acids that it requires.

229
00:21:23,400 --> 00:21:29,960
 Those that can't be synthesized by itself are called essential fatty acids.

230
00:21:29,960 --> 00:21:36,000
 Just like essential amino acids, essential fatty acids need to be obtained from the diet

231
00:21:36,000 --> 00:21:42,120
 that you eat, so you need to eat foods that are rich in these essential fatty acids, as

232
00:21:42,120 --> 00:21:47,680
 well as the essential amino acids we discussed previously.

233
00:21:47,680 --> 00:21:58,160
 Omega-3 and Omega-6 fatty acids are essential fatty acids, and we can get these very easily

234
00:21:58,160 --> 00:22:01,360
 by just eating fish.

235
00:22:01,360 --> 00:22:05,800
 They're thought to help to protect against heart disease, and nutritionists recommend

236
00:22:05,800 --> 00:22:10,400
 people eat about 12 ounces of fish every week.

237
00:22:10,400 --> 00:22:20,460
 Some people who don't eat much fish supplement their diets with fish oil capsules, however,

238
00:22:20,460 --> 00:22:26,040
 if you just obtain your fish oil with capsules, you're missing out on all those other vitamins

239
00:22:26,040 --> 00:22:34,360
 and nutrients that actually eating fish gives you.

240
00:22:34,360 --> 00:22:41,880
 A fatty acid tail contains carbon that's double bonded to another carbon, which fat has this

241
00:22:41,880 --> 00:22:42,880
 structure?

242
00:22:42,880 --> 00:22:49,520
 Well, it's called an unsaturated fat, and you've probably heard about these or read that name

243
00:22:49,520 --> 00:22:54,960
 on nutrition labels for the foods that you buy.

244
00:22:54,960 --> 00:22:59,280
 Here's some pictures to help you visualize that.

245
00:22:59,280 --> 00:23:04,000
 And all the carbons are single bonded to the hydrogens near them.

246
00:23:04,000 --> 00:23:09,840
 It creates a nice fatty acid chain that's just pretty linear.

247
00:23:09,840 --> 00:23:16,040
 Because of this, these types of fats, called saturated fats, have the maximum amount of

248
00:23:16,040 --> 00:23:20,560
 hydrogens possible, and they compact really easily.

249
00:23:20,560 --> 00:23:27,560
 So it's said that these types of fats, saturated fats, contribute more to problems with the

250
00:23:27,560 --> 00:23:34,600
 cardiovascular system, such as buildup of plaques in our arteries.

251
00:23:34,600 --> 00:23:41,600
 These types of fats tend to be solid at room temperature like butter and come from animals.

252
00:23:41,600 --> 00:23:47,520
 Types of fats, as you can see in the picture here, that have in the fatty acid chain some

253
00:23:47,520 --> 00:23:55,280
 carbons that are double bonded to one another and therefore less hydrogens have kinks within

254
00:23:55,280 --> 00:23:57,520
 the fatty acid chains.

255
00:23:57,520 --> 00:24:04,440
 Therefore, you see they don't sit as linear and as flat as the saturated fats do.

256
00:24:04,440 --> 00:24:11,040
 Therefore, these are called unsaturated fats because they don't sit so flat they tend to

257
00:24:11,040 --> 00:24:19,080
 be liquid at room temperature, tend to come from vegetables, like vegetable oil and from

258
00:24:19,080 --> 00:24:28,680
 fish, and they say that these do not contribute as much to cardiovascular problems as the saturated

259
00:24:28,680 --> 00:24:29,680
 fats do.

260
00:24:29,680 --> 00:24:39,640
 However, you still want to utilize moderation when eating fats.

261
00:24:39,640 --> 00:24:41,840
 Here's a chart to summarize that.

262
00:24:41,840 --> 00:24:48,280
 Saturated fats, once again, have carbons with single bonds to as many hydrogens as possible.

263
00:24:48,280 --> 00:24:53,080
 That's why they're called saturated, they're saturated with as many hydrogens as possible.

264
00:24:53,080 --> 00:24:59,080
 They lack double bonds, they're solid at room temperature, and they come from animals.

265
00:24:59,080 --> 00:25:04,680
 Unsaturated fats, because of those double bonds, between the carbons contain fewer hydrogens

266
00:25:04,680 --> 00:25:09,480
 and those double bonds cause kinks in the fatty acid chains.

267
00:25:09,480 --> 00:25:16,120
 This makes them not sit as flat and compacted together, therefore they tend to be liquid

268
00:25:16,120 --> 00:25:28,420
 at room temperature and come from plants and vegetables.

269
00:25:28,420 --> 00:25:33,000
 The more double bonds that there are in the fatty acid chains, the higher the degree of

270
00:25:33,000 --> 00:25:42,200
 unsaturation, and when fat contains many unsaturated carbons, the fat is referred to as polyunsaturated.

271
00:25:42,200 --> 00:25:53,680
 A good source of polyunsaturated fats is cooking oil, like corn oil or vegetable oil.

272
00:25:53,680 --> 00:26:00,600
 Commercial food manufacturers sometimes add hydrogen atoms to unsaturated fats by combining

273
00:26:00,600 --> 00:26:05,360
 hydrogen gas with vegetable oils under pressure.

274
00:26:05,360 --> 00:26:08,400
 That process is called hydrogenation.

275
00:26:08,400 --> 00:26:15,040
 It increases the level of saturation of the fat, or basically it adds more hydrogens and

276
00:26:15,040 --> 00:26:21,460
 breaks up some of those carbon double bonds and replaces them with hydrogen instead in

277
00:26:21,460 --> 00:26:26,880
 the fatty acid tails of the fat molecule.

278
00:26:26,880 --> 00:26:34,240
 They do that process to make the liquid actually a little more solidified and extends its shelf

279
00:26:34,240 --> 00:26:41,520
 life a bit, so margarine is a vegetable oil that has undergone hydrogenation.

280
00:26:41,520 --> 00:26:49,440
 Unfortunately, this process creates something called trans fats.

281
00:26:49,440 --> 00:26:54,360
 They're produced by incomplete hydrogenation, which also changes the structure of the fatty

282
00:26:54,360 --> 00:27:00,360
 acid tails in the fat so that even though there's mostly carbon-carbon double bonds, the fatty

283
00:27:00,360 --> 00:27:03,600
 acids are flat and not kinked.

284
00:27:03,600 --> 00:27:08,560
 For our bodies, these fats are not required, nor are they beneficial.

285
00:27:08,560 --> 00:27:12,480
 In fact, research has indicated they're very unhealthy.

286
00:27:12,480 --> 00:27:17,800
 If you choose to eat energy bars, protein bars, fiber bars, and other nutrition bars,

287
00:27:17,800 --> 00:27:25,240
 it's best to look for those that do not contain trans fats.

288
00:27:25,240 --> 00:27:30,520
 There's health risks of consuming those foods that have trans fats in them.

289
00:27:30,520 --> 00:27:37,680
 They include clogged arteries, heart disease, and increased risk of diabetes.

290
00:27:37,680 --> 00:27:44,080
 A final note on fats because they contain more stored energy program than carbs or proteins.

291
00:27:44,080 --> 00:27:50,360
 Excess fat intake is associated with several diseases, like the ones listed above, so overall

292
00:27:50,360 --> 00:27:59,320
 in your diet, try to limit fat intake and try to choose the healthier fats as well.

293
00:27:59,320 --> 00:28:04,100
 So on to micronutrients, vitamins are micronutrients.

294
00:28:04,100 --> 00:28:08,520
 Which vitamin can be synthesized by the human body?

295
00:28:08,520 --> 00:28:17,320
 The answer to that is vitamin D. Micronutrients are required in smaller quantities than macronutrients,

296
00:28:17,320 --> 00:28:21,960
 hence the prefix micro means small.

297
00:28:21,960 --> 00:28:24,760
 Micronutrients include vitamins and minerals.

298
00:28:24,760 --> 00:28:30,920
 These are not broken down by the body, they already come as a small structure.

299
00:28:30,920 --> 00:28:36,580
 They can go through cell membranes and then are readily used by cells.

300
00:28:36,580 --> 00:28:43,480
 They're also not burned for energy.

301
00:28:43,480 --> 00:28:48,600
 Vitamins, our first type of micronutrient, are organic substances, most of which the body

302
00:28:48,600 --> 00:28:52,320
 cannot synthesize on its own.

303
00:28:52,320 --> 00:28:57,760
 Vitamins in the body, most vitamins function as what's called a coenzyme.

304
00:28:57,760 --> 00:29:05,520
 A coenzyme is a molecule that helps enzymes and thus helps to speed up that enzymes work

305
00:29:05,520 --> 00:29:09,680
 in completing a body's chemical reaction.

306
00:29:09,680 --> 00:29:15,240
 Vitamin deficiencies can affect every cell in the body because many different enzymes,

307
00:29:15,240 --> 00:29:20,760
 all requiring the same vitamin, are involved in numerous bodily functions.

308
00:29:20,760 --> 00:29:25,280
 Vitamins can even help protect the body against cancer and heart disease and even slows the

309
00:29:25,280 --> 00:29:30,200
 aging process.

310
00:29:30,200 --> 00:29:35,360
 Vitamin D, which is also called calcitriol, is the only vitamin that our cells and our

311
00:29:35,360 --> 00:29:37,840
 body can synthesize on their own.

312
00:29:37,840 --> 00:29:45,120
 But, there's a catch, sunlight is required for that process, so people living in climates

313
00:29:45,120 --> 00:29:52,160
 with little sunlight can more easily develop a deficiency of vitamin D in those areas

314
00:29:52,160 --> 00:29:56,760
 than those that live in places where there's plentiful sunlight.

315
00:29:56,760 --> 00:30:02,800
 Healthcare providers may recommend vitamin D supplements to these people.

316
00:30:02,800 --> 00:30:10,080
 You might also see that milk often comes supplemented with vitamin D.

317
00:30:10,080 --> 00:30:21,400
 All other vitamins then must be supplied by the foods that we eat.

318
00:30:21,400 --> 00:30:33,800
 Here's a list of some food sources and what vitamins they contain.

319
00:30:33,800 --> 00:30:37,800
 Vitamin B12 is found in chicken, fish, red meat, and dairy.

320
00:30:37,800 --> 00:30:44,200
 Vitamin C in many fruits and vegetables and meats, vitamin D in milk, egg yolk, and soy.

321
00:30:44,200 --> 00:30:48,040
 But remember, we need sunlight to complete that process.

322
00:30:48,040 --> 00:30:55,280
 Vitamin E in almonds, oils, mangoes, broccoli, and nuts, and folic acid, which is in dark

323
00:30:55,280 --> 00:31:04,760
 green vegetables, nuts, legumes, and in whole grains.

324
00:31:04,760 --> 00:31:09,920
 Water soluble vitamins are the ones that are not stored by the body.

325
00:31:09,920 --> 00:31:17,840
 So, a lack of water soluble vitamins is more likely the cause of dietary deficiencies than

326
00:31:17,840 --> 00:31:23,280
 the lack of fat soluble vitamins.

327
00:31:23,280 --> 00:31:26,120
 Vitamins B and C are water soluble.

328
00:31:26,120 --> 00:31:31,920
 They can be leached out by boiling, therefore, if you eat canned vegetables, you're probably

329
00:31:31,920 --> 00:31:38,880
 not getting a good source of B and C vitamins because the canning process requires boiling.

330
00:31:38,880 --> 00:31:43,560
 So, fresh vegetables are a better source of these.

331
00:31:43,560 --> 00:31:49,380
 Vitamins A, D, E, and K are fat soluble and build up and stored fat.

332
00:31:49,380 --> 00:31:57,760
 In excess of those vitamins accumulated in the body, though, can be toxic.

333
00:31:57,760 --> 00:32:03,280
 For those people who take megadosis through supplementation of these fat soluble vitamins,

334
00:32:03,280 --> 00:32:08,320
 they can increase the risk of contracting certain cancers, health disease, and it can

335
00:32:08,320 --> 00:32:13,400
 even lead to heart disease and death.

336
00:32:13,400 --> 00:32:16,640
 Our next micronutrients is minerals.

337
00:32:16,640 --> 00:32:22,720
 These are inorganic substances, meaning they do not contain carbon, but they're still essential

338
00:32:22,720 --> 00:32:25,000
 for many cell functions.

339
00:32:25,000 --> 00:32:30,960
 Because they lack carbon, minerals are said to be the inorganic kind.

340
00:32:30,960 --> 00:32:35,920
 They are important for proper fluid balance in muscle contraction and in conduction of

341
00:32:35,920 --> 00:32:38,640
 nerve impulses.

342
00:32:38,640 --> 00:32:45,440
 They also help us build bones and teeth.

343
00:32:45,440 --> 00:32:50,080
 Here's a list of some of the foods where you can find some essential micronutrients like

344
00:32:50,080 --> 00:32:57,680
 calcium and milk, cheese, dark green vegetables and legumes, magnesium and spinach, fish,

345
00:32:57,680 --> 00:33:04,320
 nuts, seeds, beans, and brown rice, and potassium found in potatoes, tomatoes, bananas, avocados,

346
00:33:04,320 --> 00:33:12,560
 and other fresh and dried fruits, dairy, whole grains, and meats.

347
00:33:12,560 --> 00:33:17,880
 Like most vitamins, minerals are not synthesized in the body and must be supplied through our

348
00:33:17,880 --> 00:33:21,880
 diets.

349
00:33:21,880 --> 00:33:25,000
 Calcium is a commonly supplemented mineral.

350
00:33:25,000 --> 00:33:33,000
 You can get it through different pills or chewables or even in certain health drinks.

351
00:33:33,000 --> 00:33:34,920
 Bodies need calcium.

352
00:33:34,920 --> 00:33:36,360
 It helps our blood clot.

353
00:33:36,360 --> 00:33:38,280
 It helps muscles contract.

354
00:33:38,280 --> 00:33:43,600
 It helps nerves, fire, and bones to grow and stay healthy.

355
00:33:43,600 --> 00:33:49,840
 When your dietary calcium is low, your body has to get it from somewhere, so it takes

356
00:33:49,840 --> 00:33:56,880
 it from our bones and therefore it weakens bones if we don't have enough calcium in our

357
00:33:56,880 --> 00:34:00,360
 blood and in our diet.

358
00:34:00,360 --> 00:34:05,320
 If you're not getting enough calcium in your diet, which is around a thousand milligrams

359
00:34:05,320 --> 00:34:10,800
 per day, many health care providers will recommend supplements.

360
00:34:10,800 --> 00:34:16,560
 Three ounces of yogurt, one and a half ounces of cheese, and one glass of milk together

361
00:34:16,560 --> 00:34:25,760
 contain around 1,000 milligrams of calcium.

362
00:34:25,760 --> 00:34:32,880
 In addition to vitamins and minerals, antioxidants are also a micronutrient.

363
00:34:32,880 --> 00:34:37,160
 Many whole foods contain molecules called these antioxidants.

364
00:34:37,160 --> 00:34:42,000
 They help to prevent cells from damage caused by molecules that are generated by normal

365
00:34:42,000 --> 00:34:43,480
 cell processes.

366
00:34:43,480 --> 00:34:49,520
 These normal molecules are called free radicals and they're highly reactive molecules.

367
00:34:49,520 --> 00:34:57,080
 They usually have an incomplete outer electron shell, which makes them more chemically reactive

368
00:34:57,080 --> 00:35:00,680
 than other molecules with complete electron shells.

369
00:35:00,680 --> 00:35:05,600
 Free radicals can then go and damage cell membranes, the lining of arteries, and even

370
00:35:05,600 --> 00:35:08,480
 our DNA.

371
00:35:08,480 --> 00:35:12,880
 Antioxidants can inhibit chemical reactions that involve free radicals and decrease the

372
00:35:12,880 --> 00:35:16,960
 damage that they do in our cells.

373
00:35:16,960 --> 00:35:25,360
 Antioxidants are abundant in fruits, vegetables, nuts, grains, and other meats.

374
00:35:25,360 --> 00:35:31,840
 Here's a chart showing some foods that have high levels of antioxidants.

375
00:35:31,840 --> 00:35:37,340
 Kinds are beta-carotene, flavanol, and lycopene.

376
00:35:37,340 --> 00:35:44,160
 I like the middle one there with flavanol found in cocoa and dark chocolate.

377
00:35:44,160 --> 00:35:52,320
 All in all, it seems that the best benefit of antioxidants comes from eating whole foods

378
00:35:52,320 --> 00:35:54,840
 and not supplements.

379
00:35:54,840 --> 00:36:01,440
 This is mostly likely due to the fact that there needs to be a balance of both antioxidants

380
00:36:01,440 --> 00:36:07,640
 and free radicals within our bodies to maintain homeostasis and optimal health.

381
00:36:07,640 --> 00:36:13,320
 It is found that some free radicals in our body are needed to be able to kill cancer

382
00:36:13,320 --> 00:36:16,400
 cells, bacteria, and invaders.

383
00:36:16,400 --> 00:36:22,680
 So there is that balance between having the right amount of antioxidants and free radicals

384
00:36:22,680 --> 00:36:25,320
 that keeps us healthy.

385
00:36:25,320 --> 00:36:29,560
 Next, let's talk about cells.

386
00:36:29,560 --> 00:36:34,680
 Some supplements are composed of extracts produced by grinding and breaking open plant

387
00:36:34,680 --> 00:36:40,640
 cells, while there are other supplements that are made to affect a particular subcellular

388
00:36:40,640 --> 00:36:44,160
 structure within human cells.

389
00:36:44,160 --> 00:36:49,240
 As we learned in our last chapter, cells of the basic unit of life and plant and animal

390
00:36:49,240 --> 00:36:55,760
 cells evolved from a common ancestor, therefore they share many characteristics in common,

391
00:36:55,760 --> 00:37:02,840
 including their cell structures like ribosomes and organelles like mitochondria.

392
00:37:02,840 --> 00:37:08,120
 So first we'll take a look at cells and their structures, their organelles, and then we'll

393
00:37:08,120 --> 00:37:14,080
 examine some supplements made from plant cells and some supplements that inhibit organelle

394
00:37:14,080 --> 00:37:18,200
 function.

395
00:37:18,200 --> 00:37:24,560
 Cells, the basic unit of life, contain cytoplasm.

396
00:37:24,560 --> 00:37:29,880
 Cytoplasm includes two parts, cytosol, which is the liquid, and then the organelles, which

397
00:37:29,880 --> 00:37:34,440
 are the structures that are in that liquid.

398
00:37:34,440 --> 00:37:36,360
 Organelles literally means little organ.

399
00:37:36,360 --> 00:37:41,960
 They perform specific jobs that are required by the cells.

400
00:37:41,960 --> 00:37:45,600
 They also work with other organelles.

401
00:37:45,600 --> 00:37:52,080
 Cytazol is the watery matrix, and it has many different things dissolved within it, like

402
00:37:52,080 --> 00:38:00,720
 salts and enzymes, the cytosol houses the organelles.

403
00:38:00,720 --> 00:38:08,600
 All cells, no matter if you're a plant, animal, bacteria, fungus or protists, all cells contain

404
00:38:08,600 --> 00:38:12,160
 a cell membrane, also called the plasma membrane.

405
00:38:12,160 --> 00:38:18,280
 It encloses the cell and defines the outer boundary of the cell from the internal environment

406
00:38:18,280 --> 00:38:22,720
 of the cell.

407
00:38:22,720 --> 00:38:28,480
 Cell membrane's job is very important, besides being the boundary from the external and internal

408
00:38:28,480 --> 00:38:30,040
 environment of the cell.

409
00:38:30,040 --> 00:38:35,760
 It determines what materials will be allowed in or out of the cell.

410
00:38:35,760 --> 00:38:43,720
 It's semi-permeable, meaning some molecules are able to cross, and those that can't are

411
00:38:43,720 --> 00:38:49,840
 prevented from crossing.

412
00:38:49,840 --> 00:38:54,680
 As we learned in our last chapter, cell membranes are made out of a type of lipid called the

413
00:38:54,680 --> 00:38:55,840
 phospholipid.

414
00:38:55,840 --> 00:39:03,080
 That's a type of lipid that has a glycerol and a phosphate head, and then two fatty acid

415
00:39:03,080 --> 00:39:04,640
 tails.

416
00:39:04,640 --> 00:39:09,640
 One of them is saturated, and the other one is unsaturated, and you can tell that in

417
00:39:09,640 --> 00:39:13,360
 the fact that it's kinked.

418
00:39:13,360 --> 00:39:21,440
 The end of the head with the phosphate group has a charge, which makes it hydrophilic.

419
00:39:21,440 --> 00:39:28,020
 This end does not care that it's sticking into the water of the cytoplasm, the cytosol,

420
00:39:28,020 --> 00:39:30,320
 or in the extracellular fluid.

421
00:39:30,320 --> 00:39:32,960
 It does not repel that water.

422
00:39:32,960 --> 00:39:35,440
 Hydrophilic means water-loving.

423
00:39:35,440 --> 00:39:43,200
 The tail ends with the fatty acid chains are not polar or not charged, therefore they stick

424
00:39:43,200 --> 00:39:48,120
 inward into two layers, which is called the phospholipid bilayer.

425
00:39:48,120 --> 00:39:53,400
 As you can see in this picture here, there's two layers of these phospholipids, but the

426
00:39:53,400 --> 00:40:01,040
 tail end is hydrophobic, because it does not have a charge, which most lipids do not,

427
00:40:01,040 --> 00:40:06,400
 and therefore they don't want to be touching that water that's within the cytosol or in

428
00:40:06,400 --> 00:40:14,280
 the extracellular fluid, so they stick inward.

429
00:40:14,280 --> 00:40:20,200
 The cell membrane is said to have fluid properties, which allows these lipids to kind of slide

430
00:40:20,200 --> 00:40:22,800
 laterally and change places.

431
00:40:22,800 --> 00:40:28,640
 There's also proteins and such that are embedded in the cell membrane as well.

432
00:40:28,640 --> 00:40:34,280
 As you can see in the picture here, these proteins have very special jobs.

433
00:40:34,280 --> 00:40:38,960
 They often carry out enzymatic functions or certain chemical reactions.

434
00:40:38,960 --> 00:40:45,840
 They might serve as a receptor to receive different molecules, like a hormone.

435
00:40:45,840 --> 00:40:53,160
 They may also help transport things like substances across the cell membrane, things that can't

436
00:40:53,160 --> 00:40:58,960
 dissolve through the phospholipid bilayer, generally then would go through one of these

437
00:40:58,960 --> 00:41:02,960
 proteins.

438
00:41:02,960 --> 00:41:10,360
 In this picture on this slide, we see a comparison of animal cells versus plant cells.

439
00:41:10,360 --> 00:41:15,080
 While they do look very different, they do share many things in common, such as being

440
00:41:15,080 --> 00:41:16,880
 eukaryotic.

441
00:41:16,880 --> 00:41:21,520
 They have a nucleus with DNA inside.

442
00:41:21,520 --> 00:41:26,440
 They have a nucleolus where ribosomes are made.

443
00:41:26,440 --> 00:41:31,600
 They have endoplasmic reticulum, which are like transport tubes within the cell.

444
00:41:31,600 --> 00:41:37,920
 These little folded membranes here, they contain ribosomes, which are these little dots.

445
00:41:37,920 --> 00:41:40,760
 That's where proteins are synthesized.

446
00:41:40,760 --> 00:41:46,240
 These little kidney-beam-shaped things are called mitochondria, where we take glucose

447
00:41:46,240 --> 00:41:52,520
 and make it into our energy molecule called ATP.

448
00:41:52,520 --> 00:41:58,160
 And then one of the big differences you can probably see right away is the plant cell.

449
00:41:58,160 --> 00:42:02,120
 It has this big, huge central vacuole.

450
00:42:02,120 --> 00:42:10,080
 They also have these green things called chloroplasts, which contain chlorophyll that absorb sunlight

451
00:42:10,080 --> 00:42:15,160
 and allow the process of photosynthesis to occur to be able to make glucose.

452
00:42:15,160 --> 00:42:21,800
 And then that glucose can be chained together into polysaccharides called starch and then

453
00:42:21,800 --> 00:42:25,440
 stored in the central vacuole of the plant cell.

454
00:42:25,440 --> 00:42:32,880
 So literally the cell of a plant has its own glucose stores that it can use in the mitochondria

455
00:42:32,880 --> 00:42:36,720
 then to make its own ATP.

456
00:42:36,720 --> 00:42:42,920
 Plant cells also have this extra structure out here around the membrane called the cell

457
00:42:42,920 --> 00:42:43,920
 wall.

458
00:42:43,920 --> 00:42:49,320
 Cell walls are generally made out of a sugar called cellulose that I told you about earlier

459
00:42:49,320 --> 00:42:55,560
 that we call fiber because we can't digest that cellulose.

