 The answer to that is photosynthesis.

466
00:43:30,760 --> 00:43:34,040
 So let's talk about some of these subcellular structures.

467
00:43:34,040 --> 00:43:36,480
 We've mentioned the cell wall.

468
00:43:36,480 --> 00:43:40,600
 That's the extra protection outside the plasma membrane.

469
00:43:40,600 --> 00:43:47,900
 That's fungi and bacteria and most protists have cell walls, but no animal cells have a

470
00:43:47,900 --> 00:43:49,640
 cell wall.

471
00:43:49,640 --> 00:43:53,760
 We just have cell membranes around our cells.

472
00:43:53,760 --> 00:43:59,840
 The nucleus in eukaryotic cells holds DNA.

473
00:43:59,840 --> 00:44:05,440
 Those DNA also consist of proteins that they wrap around to become chromosomes.

474
00:44:05,440 --> 00:44:10,560
 So together the DNA and the proteins are called chromatin.

475
00:44:10,560 --> 00:44:14,720
 This is then surrounded by the nuclear envelope.

476
00:44:14,720 --> 00:44:23,480
 The nucleolus is within the nucleus and this is the place where ribosomes are synthesized.

477
00:44:23,480 --> 00:44:31,680
 Then they're sent out of the nucleus later on, where their job will be to make proteins.

478
00:44:31,680 --> 00:44:39,600
 The mitochondria are an organelle that use glucose to make ATP, our own energy molecules,

479
00:44:39,600 --> 00:44:43,280
 adenosine triphosphate.

480
00:44:43,280 --> 00:44:52,000
 The process of using glucose to make ATP is called cell respiration.

481
00:44:52,000 --> 00:44:58,840
 Chloroplasts are within plant cells and also photosynthetic bacteria and protists as well,

482
00:44:58,840 --> 00:45:00,680
 like algae.

483
00:45:00,680 --> 00:45:08,600
 Chloroplasts can absorb sunlight and then use that energy and other things like carbon dioxide

484
00:45:08,600 --> 00:45:18,960
 to make sugar in the form of glucose and then they can store that later to make ATP.

485
00:45:18,960 --> 00:45:27,320
 That process of absorbing sunlight and using carbon dioxide to make sugar is called photosynthesis.

486
00:45:27,320 --> 00:45:36,000
 Only organisms that have chloroplasts with the pigment chlorophyll are able to do this.

487
00:45:36,000 --> 00:45:42,520
 A lysosome, shown here, is just basically a little bubble or a vesicle that contains digestive

488
00:45:42,520 --> 00:45:44,200
 enzymes.

489
00:45:44,200 --> 00:45:53,240
 The cell uses these lysosomes as kind of like housekeepers or janitors to engulf and digest

490
00:45:53,240 --> 00:46:03,800
 enzymes to help recycle molecules and they'll also engulf and digest old cell parts as well.

491
00:46:03,800 --> 00:46:10,760
 Photosomes are really tiny, again they're made in the nucleolus, then sent out and usually

492
00:46:10,760 --> 00:46:18,360
 attached to the ER though some are free-floating in the cytosol and they make proteins.

493
00:46:18,360 --> 00:46:25,360
 Their job is to string amino acids together in the right order according to DNA's code

494
00:46:25,360 --> 00:46:30,040
 so that we can make proteins correctly.

495
00:46:30,040 --> 00:46:37,240
 There's two types of endoplasmic reticulum or ER for short, rough and smooth.

496
00:46:37,240 --> 00:46:44,640
 The rough ER is usually found more near the nucleus in a cell and usually again has ribosomes

497
00:46:44,640 --> 00:46:48,160
 attached to it.

498
00:46:48,160 --> 00:46:52,680
 The smooth ER is usually found a little further out than the rough ER.

499
00:46:52,680 --> 00:46:58,600
 They do not contain ribosomes hence they look smoother.

500
00:46:58,600 --> 00:47:04,080
 They're involved in lipid synthesis and also detoxification and both of them are basically

501
00:47:04,080 --> 00:47:09,720
 membranes that are kind of folded into little tubes and act as kind of a transport network

502
00:47:09,720 --> 00:47:12,040
 within the cell.

503
00:47:12,040 --> 00:47:20,240
 The Golgi looks a lot like the ER in the fact that their little membranes folded into tubes

504
00:47:20,240 --> 00:47:26,760
 but they're usually further out from the nucleus, further out than the ER and closer to the

505
00:47:26,760 --> 00:47:28,280
 cell membrane.

506
00:47:28,280 --> 00:47:34,200
 They're kind of like the FedEx or the packing and shipping of the cell.

507
00:47:34,200 --> 00:47:40,960
 Their job is to finish off proteins to modify them, the final modification and then package

508
00:47:40,960 --> 00:47:48,720
 them into a vesicle by bubbling off a piece of itself as you see in the picture here and

509
00:47:48,720 --> 00:47:54,840
 then shipping that protein to where it needs to go.

510
00:47:54,840 --> 00:47:58,280
 The cereals are kind of neat looking little structures.

511
00:47:58,280 --> 00:48:04,280
 They're made out of protein microtubules that have an interesting shape to them.

512
00:48:04,280 --> 00:48:10,000
 We start off in the cell with a pair of them but that soon duplicates and when a cell is

513
00:48:10,000 --> 00:48:15,600
 ready to divide then we'll have a couple pair that migrate to opposite ends of the cell and

514
00:48:15,600 --> 00:48:22,960
 help the cell go through a process called mitosis or cell division.

515
00:48:22,960 --> 00:48:27,600
 There's also other cytoskeleton elements.

516
00:48:27,600 --> 00:48:33,120
 These are structural supports for the cell and they also help in mobility of the cell

517
00:48:33,120 --> 00:48:34,120
 as well.

518
00:48:34,120 --> 00:48:40,680
 There's different sized protein filaments here from microfilaments to intermediate filaments

519
00:48:40,680 --> 00:48:44,240
 and microtubules.

520
00:48:44,240 --> 00:48:57,760
 The central vacuole which is found in a plant cell stores both water and sugars and pigments.

521
00:48:57,760 --> 00:49:06,760
 That's a very quick rundown of the different cell components and organelles.

522
00:49:06,760 --> 00:49:15,280
 Speaking of plant cells, chlorophyll supplements are marketed as being able to energize detoxify

