 Scientists use the asterisks to show that a difference is statistically significant.

549
00:51:46,400 --> 00:51:53,440
 While weather ingested in food or supplemented in pills, one substances reach cells to be

550
00:51:53,440 --> 00:51:59,200
 used they must go across the cell membrane.

551
00:51:59,200 --> 00:52:03,880
 Molecules must cross that plasma membrane to gain access to the inside of the cell where

552
00:52:03,880 --> 00:52:09,840
 they can be used by those cell structures and organelles to synthesize cell components

553
00:52:09,840 --> 00:52:15,720
 or to be metabolized to provide energy for the cell in the form of ATP.

554
00:52:15,720 --> 00:52:22,120
 The chemistry of the membrane helps to facilitate the transport of some substances but then it

555
00:52:22,120 --> 00:52:27,580
 prevents the transport of others.

556
00:52:27,580 --> 00:52:33,280
 Substances that are able to cross reach what's called an equilibrium meaning they're equal

557
00:52:33,280 --> 00:52:40,480
 concentrations on both sides of the cell membrane.

558
00:52:40,480 --> 00:52:47,800
 Remember the plasma membrane consists of the phospholipid bilayer is differential permeable

559
00:52:47,800 --> 00:52:53,080
 meaning it will allow some things across but not all.

560
00:52:53,080 --> 00:52:58,920
 Hydrophobic substances which have characteristics more like fat meaning they're non-polar pass

561
00:52:58,920 --> 00:53:10,520
 through more easily especially through that hydrophobic interior than those that are hydrophilic.

562
00:53:10,520 --> 00:53:17,960
 Diseases like carbon dioxide, water and oxygen move freely across the membrane.

563
00:53:17,960 --> 00:53:25,080
 Larger molecules and charged molecules and ions cannot cross the lipid bilayer on their

564
00:53:25,080 --> 00:53:26,560
 own.

565
00:53:26,560 --> 00:53:31,000
 If these substances are to be moved across the membrane they usually need to go through

566
00:53:31,000 --> 00:53:37,320
 a protein which is embedded in the membrane so we would say that protein serves as a channel

567
00:53:37,320 --> 00:53:43,680
 or a carrier to allow those molecules to cross until again their concentration is equal on

568
00:53:43,680 --> 00:53:48,280
 both sides or they've reached equilibrium.

569
00:53:48,280 --> 00:53:54,280
 In cases where substances go across the cell membrane against their concentration gradient

570
00:53:54,280 --> 00:54:03,680
 proteins can serve as pumps to drive molecules away from equilibrium.

571
00:54:03,680 --> 00:54:09,760
 In passive transport molecules move down their concentration gradient without the use of

572
00:54:09,760 --> 00:54:16,960
 any extra energy meaning no energy molecules in the form of ATP or dentezing triphosphate

573
00:54:16,960 --> 00:54:21,160
 are needed for this process to happen.

574
00:54:21,160 --> 00:54:26,960
 Simple diffusion is one of these processes that's how carbon dioxide moves from where

575
00:54:26,960 --> 00:54:34,520
 it's more concentrated to where it's less concentrated across a cell membrane.

576
00:54:34,520 --> 00:54:39,320
 Here we see a closer look at that.

577
00:54:39,320 --> 00:54:44,840
 Carbon dioxide are very small hydrophobic molecules and therefore they go across that

578
00:54:44,840 --> 00:54:48,440
 membrane relatively easily.

579
00:54:48,440 --> 00:54:53,320
 See how there's more carbon dioxide and more oxygen on this side of the membrane.

580
00:54:53,320 --> 00:55:03,000
 They're moving from high concentration down their concentration gradient to low concentration.

581
00:55:03,000 --> 00:55:11,920
 Facilitated diffusion is another way that substances get across the cell membrane.

582
00:55:11,920 --> 00:55:19,000
 So a facilitator is somebody that helps a process occur and so proteins are the facilitator

583
00:55:19,000 --> 00:55:26,620
 in this process proteins facilitate the movement of certain substances down their concentration

584
00:55:26,620 --> 00:55:30,640
 gradient across the cell membrane.

585
00:55:30,640 --> 00:55:36,120
 Though things are going through a protein which acts as a channel or a carrier there's

586
00:55:36,120 --> 00:55:42,760
 no extra energy that's needed for this process because things are still moving down the concentration

587
00:55:42,760 --> 00:55:51,160
 gradient from where they're more highly concentrated to where they're lowly concentrated.

588
00:55:51,160 --> 00:55:57,040
 This might help with hydrophilic substances that would not go through the cell membrane

589
00:55:57,040 --> 00:56:03,640
 by themselves because of their properties so they go through the protein instead.

590
00:56:03,640 --> 00:56:09,640
 Again no extra energy is needed it's just the fact that they have to go through a protein

591
00:56:09,640 --> 00:56:17,360
 because the lipid bilayer won't let them through.

592
00:56:17,360 --> 00:56:23,760
 Osmosis is a special type of passive transport very similar to simple diffusion but it refers

593
00:56:23,760 --> 00:56:30,480
 specifically to the movement of water across the cell membrane.

594
00:56:30,480 --> 00:56:37,240
 So osmosis is the diffusion of water across the cell membrane from high to low concentration.

595
00:56:37,240 --> 00:56:45,200
 I like to say it's going from high water concentration to low water concentration.

596
00:56:45,200 --> 00:56:51,440
 Water likes to move from where there's a lot of water to where there's less water.

597
00:56:51,440 --> 00:56:59,160
 This can be a problem for some cells for instance how about an amoeba which is a type of protest

598
00:56:59,160 --> 00:57:05,880
 that lives in a fresh water pond an amoeba within its cytoplasm and cytosol would have

599
00:57:05,880 --> 00:57:11,880
 a lot of nutrients and substances and ions within itself.

600
00:57:11,880 --> 00:57:18,800
 And then there would be less of these things outside in the fresh water beyond so water

601
00:57:18,800 --> 00:57:27,480
 from the pond tends to move into an amoeba cell from where there's high water concentration

602
00:57:27,480 --> 00:57:32,840
 out here in the pond to where there's a lower water concentration in the cytoplasm of the

603
00:57:32,840 --> 00:57:33,840
 amoeba cell.

604
00:57:33,840 --> 00:57:40,320
 Now if that kept happening that amoeba could take on too much water and then burst luckily

605
00:57:40,320 --> 00:57:51,520
 the amoeba makes little packages that grab onto that excess water and then dump it out.

606
00:57:51,520 --> 00:58:03,680
 So once again the three types of passive transport diffusion facilitated diffusion and osmosis.

607
00:58:03,680 --> 00:58:09,920
 Where none of these take any extra energy in the form of ATP and all three of them the

608
00:58:09,920 --> 00:58:16,840
 substances move from higher to lower concentration.

609
00:58:16,840 --> 00:58:22,120
 Here's some questions which process uses transport proteins to move hydrophilic molecules through

610
00:58:22,120 --> 00:58:24,120
 the plasma membrane.

611
00:58:24,120 --> 00:58:31,440
 So the answer to that is facilitated diffusion.

612
00:58:31,440 --> 00:58:36,360
 How does water move if a cell is placed in salt water?

613
00:58:36,360 --> 00:58:45,220
 Well this can be a concern especially if organisms live in salt water or when we use IV fluids

614
00:58:45,220 --> 00:58:49,080
 in the medical industry.

615
00:58:49,080 --> 00:58:55,800
 Osmosis can cause water to move out of a cell if you place that cell in salt water because

616
00:58:55,800 --> 00:59:02,000
 there's more water in the cell and less water in the salt water.

617
00:59:02,000 --> 00:59:07,800
 Water will want to move out of the cell into that salt water down its water concentration

618
00:59:07,800 --> 00:59:11,600
 gradient.

619
00:59:11,600 --> 00:59:19,200
 Because of this when we use IVs in the medical industry we have to make sure that the IV fluids

620
00:59:19,200 --> 00:59:25,280
 have the same concentration of solutes as our fluids in our body.

621
00:59:25,280 --> 00:59:38,920
 That way we don't get cells taking on excess water or losing water.

622
00:59:38,920 --> 00:59:46,680
 A type of transport across membranes that does take energy is called active transport.

623
00:59:46,680 --> 00:59:55,760
 In order to power this process ATP or adenosine triphosphate is needed.

624
00:59:55,760 --> 01:00:05,920
 ATP activates the proteins that then act as a pump to pump molecules or substances against

625
01:00:05,920 --> 01:00:09,000
 their concentration gradient.

626
01:00:09,000 --> 01:00:14,600
 So in the picture here you see a potassium ion being pumped by this protein in a cell

627
01:00:14,600 --> 01:00:21,480
 membrane using ATP of course from where it's slowly concentrated to where it's more highly

628
01:00:21,480 --> 01:00:22,800
 concentrated.

629
01:00:22,800 --> 01:00:29,840
 So it's going against its concentration gradient that's going to take energy and yes that has

630
01:00:29,840 --> 01:00:41,800
 to happen often in our cells so cells do have to use energy to move things in and out.

631
01:00:41,800 --> 01:00:45,900
 So which one here describes active transport?

632
01:00:45,900 --> 01:00:54,880
 It's letter B. Potassium will move from low concentration to high concentration using ATP.

633
01:00:54,880 --> 01:01:01,360
 A common process using quite a bit of energy daily in our bodies doing just this is called

634
01:01:01,360 --> 01:01:04,840
 the sodium potassium pump.

635
01:01:04,840 --> 01:01:12,680
 These proteins constantly have to move sodium and potassium to their proper place on their

636
01:01:12,680 --> 01:01:21,920
 proper sides of the cells to maintain the charge on the inside and outside of cells.

637
01:01:21,920 --> 01:01:28,120
 This uses quite a bit of ATP each day just for this sodium potassium pump alone which

638
01:01:28,120 --> 01:01:35,220
 is a form of active transport.

639
01:01:35,220 --> 01:01:40,640
 Sometimes things are bigger than just little molecules moving in and out of cells.

640
01:01:40,640 --> 01:01:49,640
 Sometimes we have to get big things into or out of a cell therefore we need other processes.

641
01:01:49,640 --> 01:01:52,400
 One of those is called exocytosis.

642
01:01:52,400 --> 01:01:59,760
 Like ex or exit, these things are exiting the cell or leaving the cell.

643
01:01:59,760 --> 01:02:05,960
 So things are packaged into a little vesicle, a vesicle that contains phospholipids.

